Does requiring one weighting to explain the whole table let a reader
predict a row that weighting was not fitted to?

three-coordinate table (time, bytes, spread), p1
  regions 6, arms 6, sections at the strictly-positive rung: 58
  Pareto-admissible arms per region (the baseline a reader already has): [4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 2]

  leave-one-out trials: 348
    determinate     162    46.55%
    narrowed        168    48.28%
    vacuous          18     5.17%
  mean surviving arms at the hidden region: 1.621
  mean admissible arms at the hidden region: 3.667
  hidden row recovered uniquely and correctly: 162 of 348 (46.55%)

97's two-coordinate model (ns per record, bits), p6
  regions 6, arms 5, sections at the strictly-positive rung: 9
  Pareto-admissible arms per region (the baseline a reader already has): [3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 2]

  leave-one-out trials: 54
    determinate      38    70.37%
    narrowed          8    14.81%
    vacuous           8    14.81%
  mean surviving arms at the hidden region: 1.296
  mean admissible arms at the hidden region: 2.333
  hidden row recovered uniquely and correctly: 38 of 54 (70.37%)

reading
  The constraint is not a lookup-key-versus-rule dichotomy. It buys a
  narrowing, and how much it buys is a property of the table rather
  than of the constraint. Where a strategy's remaining freedom at the
  hidden region is more than one arm, the name is predictive about
  which arms are POSSIBLE and silent about which is taken, which is
  weaker than the argument for the constraint claims and stronger
  than nothing.

  Two honest bounds on this probe. Leave-one-out over the regions a
  table was fitted on is not the same as a genuinely new region with
  costs nobody has seen, and the second is what the argument is about;
  this measures the first because the second needs a measurement that
  does not exist. And a determinate rate is a fact about these two
  tables, which p3 showed varies by a factor of tens across tables of
  the same shape.
